<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Magne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maina Castle, Castle of Grand Magne, Maina castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Castle of Grand Magne was a fortress built around 1248 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> peninsula in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, to control the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a>. After his capture at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, William ceded it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 1262 as his ransom. Its probable site is at Cape Tigani in the Mani.</p></div>
